        "title": "CPU throttling",
        "source": "cpu-throttling.md",
        "section": "CPU throttling",
        "text": "# CPU throttling\n\nThe container hits its CPU `limit` and the kernel's CFS quota pauses it for the rest of each 100 ms period. Nothing crashes and the pod stays `Running` and `Ready`, but work runs slowly: latency goes up, batches take longer, and probes with short timeouts may start to fail.",
        "score": 0.8284188535695821,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "CPU throttling",
        "source": "cpu-throttling.md",
        "section": "Diagnosis",
        "text": "## Diagnosis\n\n1. Read the requests and limits:\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl get pod <pod> -n <ns> -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].resources}'\n   ```\n\n2. Compare usage with the limit:\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl top pod <pod> -n <ns> --containers\n   ```\n\n3. Check throttling in Prometheus:\n\n   ```promql\n   sum by (container) (rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total{namespace=\"<ns>\", pod=\"<pod>\"}[5m]))\n     / sum by (container) (rate(container_cpu_cfs_periods_total{namespace=\"<ns>\", pod=\"<pod>\"}[5m]))\n   ```\n\n4. If a rollout changed the resources, compare revisions with `kubectl rollout history deploy/<name> -n <ns> --revision=<n>`.",
        "score": 0.8031627656686282,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "CPU throttling",
        "source": "cpu-throttling.md",
        "section": "Fixes",
        "text": "## Fixes\n\n- Raise the CPU `limit` (or remove it and rely on `requests` for scheduling) to a value measured from real usage plus headroom.\n- If a recent rollout lowered the limit, roll back to the previous revision, then fix the manifest.\n- Scale out (more replicas) when the work can be spread across pods; this does not make a single slow request faster.\n- Size runtime thread pools to the cgroup quota (`GOMAXPROCS`, `-XX:ActiveProcessorCount`, worker counts).\n- Raise probe `timeoutSeconds` if probes fail only because the container is throttled.",
        "score": 0.7692609251909673,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "CPU throttling",
        "source": "cpu-throttling.md",
        "section": "Symptoms",
        "text": "## Symptoms\n\n- The pod looks healthy: no restarts, no warning events, no errors in the logs.\n- CPU usage sits at (or just under) the container's CPU limit, for example `20m` used of a `20m` limit.\n- A large share of CFS periods are throttled: `container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total / container_cpu_cfs_periods_total` above roughly 25%.\n- Logs show work taking longer than usual (slow batches, slow requests, timeouts to callers), not failing outright.\n- Liveness or readiness probes with tight `timeoutSeconds` may fail intermittently under load.",
        "score": 0.7546078844701993,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "CPU throttling",
        "source": "cpu-throttling.md",
        "section": "Common causes",
        "text": "## Common causes\n\n- The CPU `limit` is too low for the workload (tiny demo limits such as `10m` to `50m` on a CPU-bound process).\n- A rollout lowered `resources.limits.cpu`, or added a limit where there was none.\n- Traffic or batch size grew while the limit stayed the same.\n- Multi-threaded runtimes (JVM, Go, Node worker pools) that size their thread pools from the node's cores, not the cgroup quota, burn the quota in a few milliseconds.",
        "score": 0.7457080185120177,
        "kind": "runbook"
      }
    ],
